{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "ui": "tui",
  "globalDependencies": [".env"],
  // Bun loads the repo-root .env before invoking Turbo. Strict environment
  // mode then exposes only variables declared here (or on an individual task),
  // so list the public values that can affect generated application output.
  "globalEnv": [
    "NODE_ENV",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY",
    // SEO, phase 3. Both are inlined at build time and both change generated
    // output — the first is the origin every canonical, sitemap entry and
    // JSON-LD @id is built from, the second is the ADR 018 index gate that
    // decides whether robots.txt says `Disallow: /`. Undeclared, strict mode
    // would hide them from `next build` and a cutover build would quietly
    // ship the pre-cutover robots.txt from cache.
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_INDEXABLE"
  ],
  // Server and CLI credentials still need to reach the app processes, but they
  // are runtime inputs rather than public values baked into client bundles.
  "globalPassThroughEnv": [
    // Runtime authorization input for the native upload and Convex admin
    // boundaries. It is never bundled into client output.
    "ADMIN_CLERK_USER_ID",
    "CLERK_SECRET_KEY",
    "CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT",
    "CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY",
    "UPLOADTHING_TOKEN",
    // Ask Corey's answering half. `OPENAI_API_KEY` is the one provider key the
    // feature needs — the same credential the Convex deployment holds for
    // embeddings, read here by `/api/ask` at request time. Strict environment
    // mode is Turbo 2's default, so a variable that is in the root .env but not
    // named anywhere in this file never reaches `next dev`, `next build` or
    // `next start`: the route would read `undefined` and answer 503
    // "OPENAI_API_KEY is unset" on a machine where the key is plainly set.
    //
    // Pass-through rather than `globalEnv` so that rotating the key does not
    // invalidate every cached build.
    //
    // ⚠️ CAVEAT, measured 2026-07-31 — the usual justification for pass-through
    // ("a runtime secret is never baked into generated output") is NOT fully
    // true here, and the difference is worth knowing before trusting a cache.
    // `(site)/layout.tsx` calls `answeringConfigured()`, a `process.env
    // .OPENAI_API_KEY` read, and passes the boolean to `<AskLauncher>`. Every
    // `(site)` route is statically prerendered, so that boolean IS baked into
    // build output. Demonstrated both ways on this tree:
    //
    //   build WITH the key     → .next/server/app/index.html
    //                            carries `answeringConfigured":true`
    //   build with NO env      → same file carries `false`
    //   build with full env
    //     MINUS only this key  → **turbo cache hit** → restores the `true`
    //                            output, because a pass-through variable is
    //                            excluded from the hash by definition.
    //
    // So a build produced on a keyed machine and replayed on a keyless one
    // ships a widget seeded "configured" that then 503s on the first question.
    // Three things keep that from being a live bug, and they are the reason
    // this is a caveat and not a fix:
    //
    //   1. the route's `503 { configured: false }` is authoritative and
    //      overrides the seeded state the moment anyone asks (see `AskPanel`);
    //   2. `/` is ISR at 300s, so a running server re-renders the boolean off
    //      the real environment within five minutes of a deploy;
    //   3. in practice the key is set in both places or neither.
    //
    // Promote this to `globalEnv` if (1)–(3) ever stop holding — the cost is
    // exactly the cache invalidation-on-rotation this line was written to
    // avoid, so it is a trade, not an oversight.
    "OPENAI_API_KEY",
    // Optional override for the answering model id (an OpenAI id). Same
    // reasoning: read per request, so it has to survive strict mode.
    "ASK_MODEL",
    // Salt for the rate-limit identifier digest (`requestIdentity.ts`). Listed
    // for exactly the reason above and documented in packages/convex/README.md
    // as a root-`.env` variable: without this line, strict mode meant the
    // documented setup did nothing on a local `bun dev`/`bun build`, and the
    // keyed run still logged "[rate-limit] RATE_LIMIT_SALT is not set".
    //
    // Pass-through, not `globalEnv`: the salt is read per request inside the
    // route handler and never reaches generated output, so it neither belongs
    // in the build hash nor should rotating it invalidate every cached build.
    // (Contrast the OPENAI_API_KEY caveat above, where a *derived boolean* does
    // get prerendered — no equivalent leak exists for the salt, which is read
    // only on the request path.)
    "RATE_LIMIT_SALT"
  ],
  "tasks": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      // The public variables declared globally above are included in the build
      // hash when they come from the root .env. Keep package-local env files as
      // inputs too, so a developer override loaded directly by Next also
      // invalidates that package's cached output. `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` preserves
      // the normal source-file inputs.
      "inputs": ["$TURBO_DEFAULT$", ".env", ".env.*"],
      "outputs": [".next/**", "!.next/cache/**", "dist/**"]
    },
    // packages/convex's `codegen` script. Declared so `turbo run codegen`
    // resolves from the root rather than erroring, and uncached because it
    // writes into the source tree (`convex/_generated`) and needs a configured,
    // reachable deployment — a cache replay can honour neither.
    //
    // Deliberately nothing `dependsOn` it. Making `typecheck` or `build` wait on
    // codegen would make both unrunnable on a checkout with no Convex project,
    // which is exactly why `convex/_generated` is committed instead. See
    // packages/convex/README.md.
    "codegen": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "dev": {
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true
    },
    "lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^lint"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^test"]
    },
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^typecheck"]
    }
  }
}
